  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "How to Train Your Decision Tree",
      "name": "How to Train Your Decision Tree",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students take on the role of data scientists working with the previsualization team from the Amazon Studios career tour. This team decides which movie scenes get a 3D rough draft before filming. After meeting the team in a short video, students explore a dataset of past scenes labeled Rough Draft or Just Film It. They train various decision trees on the same scenes using different features, and compare what makes a scene risky to film. They finish by naming an assumption the data makes about real movies.",
        "student_overview": "You’ll work as a data scientist helping a movie team decide which scenes need a rough draft before filming. You’ll train decision trees using different features and find out which ones help the model make good choices. Then your team will each add a new feature and test your trees on scenes they’ve never seen. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "How to Train Your Decision Tree",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "decision-tree-beta-test"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Testing and Tracing Predictions",
      "name": "Testing and Tracing Predictions",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students learn that a model's mistakes are not random. Every prediction a decision tree makes can be traced back to the training data that produced it. Working as data scientists for a movie studio's marketing team, students build the skill of tracing: reasoning backward from a wrong prediction to the specific examples that taught it. Through tracing, they discover that mistakes come from the data in different ways. Sometimes the data doesn't have enough of the right examples, and sometimes it never recorded the information that mattered.\n",
        "student_overview": "When an AI model makes a mistake, where does it come from? In this lesson, you'll learn to trace a model's wrong answer back to the data that taught it. You'll find out that different problems in the data cause different kinds of mistakes, and that some can be fixed by changing the data while others can't."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Testing and Tracing Predictions",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "decision-tree-beta-test"
      }
    }
  ],
